Committed. Here's what landed.

## What changed

`src/commands_search.rs` — `parse_grep_args`:

1. `--` end-of-flags terminator. A end_of_flags flag before the loop, an early continue at the top of the loop that pushes tokens straight into remaining_parts, and a "--" arm that flips the flag and consumes the terminator itself (so -- never becomes the pattern). /grep -- -C src/ now searches for the literal -C.

2. The silent swallow is gone. The three // silently ignored comments were the birthplace of a silent wrong-op: /grep "-C" src/ matched the flag arm, failed to parse src/ as a number, dropped -C on the floor, and handed the user results for src/ — a search they never asked for. Every value-taking flag (`-C/--context, -B/--before, -A/--after, --include, --exclude) now has an explicit else that pushes the flag token into remaining_parts` and does not consume the next token. The loser branch names itself instead of vanishing.

Three existing tests pinned the old behavior (..._context_without_number_ignored, `..._include_without_value_ignored, ..._exclude_without_value_ignored`). I updated them rather than deleting — each now asserts the fixed behavior with a comment naming Day 163 and #706. That's the honest shape: the old assertions were correct records of a bug.

Nine new tests, split deliberately: four on the new paths, four on normal paths that must be untouched (-C 3 foo src/, --include *.rs foo, -s/-c, -foo), one fixture loop over bare value-flags. Day 122/124's lesson is that discriminators get tested only on the side that fires — so the untouched side is pinned too.

Docs: one line in help_data.rs's /grep block, one bullet in `docs/src/usage/commands.md covering both halves. **tests/module_size.rs`:** 3602 → 3720 with a comment naming the day and reason.

## What did *not* land

The quoted-token half. tokenize_quoted still returns bare strings with no was-quoted marker, so quoting genuinely cannot escape a flag name — the fix above makes /grep "-C" src/ do the right thing by a different route, but /grep "-C" 3 would still parse as a context flag. Threading a was-quoted bit through a shared helper touches unrelated callers; that's a separate task. #706 stays open.

